# WAAMTO Enterprise QA & Certification Rules v3.0

> Mandatory QA workflow for all WAAMTO ecosystem projects.

---

# Purpose

The purpose of QA is to certify production readiness while minimising unnecessary work, repository scanning and token usage.

QA is evidence-based.

Never guess.

---

# QA Objectives

Always:

- Verify correctness.
- Validate requested functionality.
- Protect architecture.
- Detect regressions.
- Minimise risk.
- Produce a certification decision.

---

# QA Principles

QA is not development.

QA should identify issues first.

Only perform safe fixes when explicitly allowed.

---

# QA Workflow

Follow this sequence.

1. Understand scope.
2. Identify affected modules.
3. Read only required files.
4. Execute relevant validation.
5. Record findings.
6. Apply safe fixes (if permitted).
7. Re-test affected functionality.
8. Produce certification.
9. Stop.

Never continue into unrelated testing.

---

# Scope Rules

Never test the entire application unless requested.

Test only:

- affected feature
- affected module
- affected API
- affected UI
- affected workflow

---

# Repository Rules

Never scan the complete repository during QA.

Never inspect unrelated modules.

Never generate project-wide audits unless requested.

---

# Safe Fix Policy

Safe fixes include:

- obvious bug fixes
- missing validation
- incorrect imports
- configuration mistakes
- broken references
- small UI issues

Never perform:

- architecture redesign
- major refactoring
- dependency replacement
- database redesign

during QA.

---

# Regression Testing

Test only areas that could reasonably be affected.

Avoid unrelated regression testing.

---

# Test Priority

Priority order:

1. Critical functionality
2. Security
3. Data integrity
4. Business logic
5. API behaviour
6. UI behaviour
7. Performance

---

# Validation Areas

When applicable validate:

- functionality
- business rules
- API responses
- permissions
- authentication
- authorisation
- validation
- error handling
- logging
- database behaviour
- UI rendering
- responsiveness

Only validate what is relevant.

---

# Evidence

Every finding must include evidence.

Examples:

- failing endpoint
- error message
- failing test
- console error
- screenshot
- code reference

Never report assumptions.

---

# Certification Levels

Only use one of the following.

PASS

Requirements:

- requested functionality works
- no critical issues
- no high-risk defects

---

PASS WITH MINOR ISSUES

Requirements:

- functionality works
- only low-risk issues remain
- production acceptable

List remaining minor issues.

---

FAIL

Requirements:

- critical functionality broken
- data integrity risk
- security issue
- production risk
- blocking defects

Clearly explain why.

---

# Reporting Format

Always include:

## Scope

What was tested.

## Validation

Checks performed.

## Findings

Issues discovered.

## Fixes

Safe fixes applied (if any).

## Remaining Issues

Only if applicable.

## Certification

PASS

or

PASS WITH MINOR ISSUES

or

FAIL

---

# Performance

Do not run heavy performance testing unless requested.

Run only relevant performance validation.

---

# Security

Always verify relevant:

- authentication
- authorisation
- permissions
- validation
- input handling

Never bypass security checks.

---

# Database

Never modify schema during QA.

Never generate migrations during QA.

---

# Token Optimisation

Use minimum:

- searches
- file reads
- tests
- analysis

Reuse previous context whenever possible.

---

# Completion Rules

QA ends immediately after:

- certification completed
- report generated
- requested scope validated

Do not continue searching for optional improvements.

---

